WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.200
Our next speaker is Thomas Peacock, who's going to talk to us about navigating the

00:07.200 --> 00:13.000
avicapsid secret space using virus-specific protein language models.

00:13.000 --> 00:21.840
Okay, thank you, good morning everyone.

00:21.840 --> 00:27.680
My name is Thomas Peacock, I am an AI research scientist at Lear Therapeutics and yes, today

00:27.680 --> 00:32.240
I will introduce some of the work we've been doing developing virus-specific protein language

00:32.240 --> 00:37.040
models for AAV capsid design.

00:37.040 --> 00:41.840
At Lear, we are aiming to reimagine viral delivery starting with AAV.

00:41.840 --> 00:48.820
So we have developed our multi-modal lab-in-the-loop platform navigator to generate AI optimized

00:48.820 --> 00:55.000
libraries with high impact mutations, not just in a single region, but across the entire

00:55.000 --> 00:57.000
viral surface.

00:57.000 --> 01:03.280
These libraries are validated through a combination of our in vivo tissue screens for tropism

01:03.280 --> 01:09.600
and potency and in vitro assays for immunovasion, with each experiment feeding new data back

01:09.600 --> 01:17.520
into the navigator platform to retrain and refine our models.

01:17.520 --> 01:21.360
Navigator itself is a holistic AI platform for viral vectors.

01:21.360 --> 01:26.880
Some of its core components include our virus-specific DNA models that we use for library generation

01:26.880 --> 01:31.040
our structural analysis suite that we use for rapid capsid assembly and large scale

01:31.040 --> 01:35.240
docking screens and our protein language models that I will talk about throughout the rest

01:35.240 --> 01:38.520
of this talk.

01:38.520 --> 01:43.120
We've already made some exciting progress using our pipeline and even in our early results

01:43.120 --> 01:48.360
we have observed clear improvements in both enrichment and tissue detargeting across our

01:48.360 --> 01:52.600
libraries.

01:52.600 --> 01:57.320
As a brief introduction to protein language models, these are neural networks that map

01:57.320 --> 02:02.680
amino acid sequences into high dimensional vector representations to capture the patterns

02:02.680 --> 02:08.800
that link protein sequence to protein structure and protein function.

02:08.800 --> 02:14.240
To learn these patterns the model is trained on an objective like masked language modeling

02:14.240 --> 02:19.600
where it is required to predict amino acids that have been intentionally hidden in the

02:19.600 --> 02:26.280
input sequence by observing the surrounding sequence context and by iteratively updating

02:26.280 --> 02:33.640
its own weights the aim is to reduce the difference between its predictions and the true sequence.

02:33.640 --> 02:38.920
The real beauty of this strategy is that the model is learning relationships between proteins

02:38.920 --> 02:41.760
using sequence alone.

02:41.760 --> 02:46.240
This allows us to leverage the millions and millions of protein sequences that we have

02:46.320 --> 02:57.320
collected as a scientific community to learn across the full diversity of protein space.

02:57.320 --> 03:02.640
However when it comes to very specific tasks such as AAV engineering this enormous diversity

03:02.640 --> 03:05.280
becomes something of a double-edged sword.

03:05.280 --> 03:09.280
Popular protein language models like ESM2 are generalists.

03:09.280 --> 03:15.100
They are trained on representative sequences across the whole protein universe but they

03:15.140 --> 03:22.460
lack the dense coverage of AAV sequence variation that is important if we want to model for

03:22.460 --> 03:27.140
example tissue specificity or immunovasion.

03:27.140 --> 03:32.660
The plot on the left here shows a sample of the ESM2 training data with the viral sequences

03:32.660 --> 03:38.580
highlighted in pink and then on the right hand side we have subtracted the background protein

03:38.580 --> 03:44.080
families that were shown in purple and supplemented the data with additional publicly available

03:44.080 --> 03:48.160
viral sequences including AAV sequences.

03:48.160 --> 03:53.200
And it's clear I think that not only are the AAV sequences not present in the ESM2 training

03:53.200 --> 03:58.560
data but they also largely fall out of distribution of the training data.

03:58.560 --> 04:04.760
So to address this problem we have retrained the ESM2 architecture using our viral data

04:04.760 --> 04:11.760
section here to create a specialist model that is tailored to AAV engineering tasks.

04:12.760 --> 04:18.960
We have benchmarked our specialist model that we are calling Corsair against the public

04:18.960 --> 04:25.160
protein gym viral data set and we have found that Corsair shown here in green outperforms

04:25.160 --> 04:32.160
ESM2 in pink across all the metrics that we've computed and for all the model sizes that

04:32.840 --> 04:37.640
we have tested.

04:37.640 --> 04:42.520
While it is important for us to validate our models on public benchmarks our main motivation

04:42.520 --> 04:46.680
is to apply them to our own in vivo data sets.

04:46.680 --> 04:52.040
We have refined Corsair on results from tissue experiments that we have done in mice and this

04:52.040 --> 05:00.480
allows us to use Corsair directly to design our new variant libraries.

05:00.480 --> 05:05.960
To apply a protein language model to our own endpoint tasks like tissue troposome or liver

05:05.960 --> 05:12.960
avoidance we need to transfer the learned representation into our AAV predictive setting

05:12.960 --> 05:16.880
and we do that by adapting the pre-trained network that we've previously been talking

05:16.880 --> 05:21.200
about with our new label data sets.

05:21.200 --> 05:23.760
The simplest way to do this is through fine tuning.

05:23.760 --> 05:29.720
This is where we extend the network shown in pink with a task specific head shown here

05:29.720 --> 05:35.760
in green and this time we continue the training but for a regression or classification objective

05:35.760 --> 05:40.080
rather than the masked language modeling objective that we discussed earlier and we

05:40.080 --> 05:44.840
do that using our labeled examples.

05:44.840 --> 05:49.880
When fine tuning there is a key trade off between speed and performance so allowing

05:49.880 --> 05:54.640
all of the weights of the model to update offers us maximum flexibility to adapt to

05:54.640 --> 05:59.760
the new task but it is computationally expensive whereas we can freeze some of these model

05:59.760 --> 06:04.320
weights to make the model faster and lighter but it will limit the ability of the model

06:04.320 --> 06:06.160
to adapt.

06:06.160 --> 06:09.920
We've compared performance when freezing all of the weights, some of the weights and

06:09.920 --> 06:13.700
none of the weights as part of our benchmarking.

06:13.700 --> 06:17.880
We've also compared these models to the Laura fine tuning method shown on the right hand

06:17.880 --> 06:25.960
side here and under Laura we insert small trainable matrices into the model while keeping

06:25.960 --> 06:30.880
the base network frozen and it's these matrices that update through the training process rather

06:30.880 --> 06:32.600
than the full network.

06:32.600 --> 06:36.840
The aim here is essentially to act as a shortcut achieving high performance by keeping the

06:36.840 --> 06:42.200
training fast and memory efficient.

06:42.200 --> 06:46.080
The results of this benchmarking showed that while reducing the number of parameter updates

06:46.080 --> 06:51.800
through freezing does improve training efficiency it generally comes at the cost of performance.

06:51.800 --> 06:56.400
The plot here shows the effect in one of our heart tissue data sets but we see a similar

06:56.400 --> 07:02.720
pattern across tissues, across both classification and regression tasks and across different

07:02.720 --> 07:04.920
model sizes.

07:04.920 --> 07:08.840
In contrast the Laura fine tuning method tends to preserve the performance close to full

07:08.840 --> 07:13.800
fine tuning while significantly reducing training time so as a result this is generally

07:13.800 --> 07:20.880
the approach we have taken for our future model iterations.

07:20.880 --> 07:26.000
When comparing Corsair and ESM2 on our in-house tissue data sets we observe that Corsair improves

07:26.000 --> 07:28.040
performance across tissues.

07:28.040 --> 07:32.600
The radial plot on the right here shows varying spearmint correlations when predicting relative

07:32.600 --> 07:38.240
transduction across one of our recent tissue libraries and this reflects our targeted library

07:38.240 --> 07:43.960
design where in this case we intended to probe the fitness landscape of a localized region

07:43.960 --> 07:46.520
on the capsid surface.

07:46.520 --> 07:50.400
Across the panel we see a meaningful effect size improvement using Corsair which gives

07:50.400 --> 07:55.560
us a more stable foundation for modeling transduction and identifying both high and

07:55.560 --> 07:59.440
low performing candidates.

07:59.440 --> 08:03.680
While the improvement on the previous slide may appear modest I think it's worth highlighting

08:03.680 --> 08:06.680
the effect that this can have in large libraries.

08:06.680 --> 08:12.000
So here is a example, a toy simulation that we ran where we start with an initial pool

08:12.000 --> 08:17.480
of five million variants which is fairly typical of our current pipeline.

08:17.600 --> 08:24.280
If we assume that one percent of those are hits and that we have two models we are comparing,

08:24.280 --> 08:30.840
models A and B that achieve spearmint correlations of 0.50 and 0.52 respectively then when we

08:30.840 --> 08:35.040
screen the variance with those two models and we select the top 40,000 to proceed to

08:35.040 --> 08:41.320
experimental testing we find that model B identifies over 400 additional hits compared

08:41.320 --> 08:46.400
to model A so not an insignificant increase I think for just that small two percent difference

08:46.480 --> 08:51.920
in spearmint correlation.

08:51.920 --> 08:56.360
Alongside refining our models with more complete AAV data sets we are continuing to iterate

08:56.360 --> 08:59.640
on our model design to further improve performance.

08:59.640 --> 09:02.840
One recent example of this is our multi tissue model.

09:02.840 --> 09:08.520
So this builds upon the Corsair architecture but here we are training a single model jointly

09:08.520 --> 09:10.200
across all tissues.

09:10.200 --> 09:14.720
So this not only improves our training efficiency because now we are just training a single model

09:14.720 --> 09:20.800
rather than separate models for every single tissue but it also allows the model to use

09:20.800 --> 09:27.920
cross tissue patterns to hopefully improve its predictive performance.

09:27.920 --> 09:31.720
The plot on the right here again shows the spearmint correlation when predicting relative

09:31.720 --> 09:37.280
transduction but this time for our multi task model compared to our single task models in

09:37.280 --> 09:42.120
green and pink respectively and we found that the current version of our multi task Corsair

09:42.120 --> 09:50.240
model outperforms our single task models on all tissues except for liver and spleen.

09:50.240 --> 09:55.400
To sum up our protein language model module is just one component of the broader navigator

09:55.400 --> 10:01.400
platform for comprehensive modeling of the AAV virus capsid.

10:01.400 --> 10:06.080
It sits alongside our other models and our library generation tools which together enable

10:06.080 --> 10:11.440
the design of variant libraries that we validate in vivo and produce the data that drive the

10:11.440 --> 10:17.400
next generation of models which power our navigator engine.

10:17.400 --> 10:21.560
To conclude I'd like to thank the rest of the team at Lear, a special thank you to Stephen

10:21.560 --> 10:26.760
for his work on the modeling, I'll be here at ASGCT for the rest of the week along with

10:26.760 --> 10:31.120
our co-founders Kilian and Hugh so please do come and find us if you would like to know

10:31.120 --> 10:35.920
more about the work we're doing or if you are interested in potential partnerships or

10:35.920 --> 10:36.920
collaborations.

10:36.920 --> 10:39.720
Enjoy the rest of the conference and thank you very much for listening.

10:41.440 --> 10:47.960
Thank you very much.

10:47.960 --> 10:51.960
There are any questions from the audience?

10:51.960 --> 10:54.880
I have a very naive question.

10:54.880 --> 11:01.680
I think you did mention the Corsair can also be used for immunization.

11:01.680 --> 11:04.920
Have you tried any of that and have you put them to test?

11:04.920 --> 11:05.920
Yes.

11:05.920 --> 11:12.680
We have generated immune data sets and we do train or we refine the model on those data

11:12.680 --> 11:13.680
sets.

11:13.680 --> 11:22.160
We didn't have time to show any of those results today, we have shown them at a previous conference.

11:22.160 --> 11:28.680
We see kind of a similar performance I think so we are not getting like sort of perfect

11:28.680 --> 11:37.080
performance but we are identifying candidates that we can take forward to future screens.

11:37.080 --> 11:43.720
In our most recent immune study we did identify a pool of candidates that had a specific motif

11:43.720 --> 11:53.320
that was quite interesting where the variants that performed well in immunovasion sort of

11:53.320 --> 11:55.200
shared this motif.

11:55.200 --> 11:56.200
Thank you.

11:56.200 --> 11:59.360
I have a lot of questions from the audience now.

11:59.360 --> 12:02.360
Hi, I'm Jason from Sanofi, great talk.

12:02.360 --> 12:07.840
Could you reiterate some more on the training data used for training Corsair specifically

12:07.840 --> 12:09.880
for the AAV variants?

12:09.880 --> 12:14.480
Were they like all different AAV serotypes, were they all like insertions or just like

12:14.480 --> 12:18.320
slightly mutated versions of a specific serotype?

12:18.320 --> 12:28.120
So for the pre-training step which uses the huge number of unannotated sequences, ESM2

12:28.120 --> 12:37.040
uses the UNRF50 data set which essentially clusters protein sequences so you miss out

12:37.040 --> 12:43.040
on a lot of virus sequences that share similar features.

12:43.040 --> 12:47.280
So part of our data set has been just general viruses, both double stranded and single

12:47.280 --> 12:54.720
stranded viruses to keep our data set somewhat diverse rather than just AAV sequences.

12:54.720 --> 13:02.360
We have added all of the AAV wild type serotypes into the data set and then we've really looked

13:02.360 --> 13:08.840
for as much AAV data as we can find so some of the data is, well a lot of the data is

13:08.840 --> 13:13.600
AAV2 and AAV9 just because that's the sort of data that's available.

13:13.600 --> 13:17.600
We are working in AAV9 at the moment so that's sort of fine for us.

13:17.600 --> 13:20.840
How this would translate to other serotypes in the future is kind of an open question

13:20.840 --> 13:21.840
for us.

13:21.840 --> 13:26.120
Hi, thanks for the talk.

13:26.120 --> 13:30.720
I was interested in your plot where you showed that AAV lives in a different part of the

13:30.720 --> 13:33.280
latent space than the other viral sequences.

13:33.280 --> 13:36.840
There was still a lot of structure and I was wondering if that recapitulated what we know

13:36.840 --> 13:41.400
about AAV serotypes, phylogenetic relationships or even like species tropism.

13:41.400 --> 13:53.720
Yes, so I think that the way that you plot this is kind of not misleading but like I

13:53.720 --> 14:02.440
think this was not really the latent space, what we did was we split all the sequences

14:02.440 --> 14:08.960
by camers and then sort of plotted based on frequency of camers here so I think that does

14:08.960 --> 14:14.160
mean that you see this kind of distributed pattern for the AAV where you have a lot of

14:14.160 --> 14:18.440
single mutations that's spreading out the data.

14:18.440 --> 14:25.280
The majority of the ESM2 training data, the viral population is double stranded DNA viruses

14:25.280 --> 14:29.360
so I think this is why it clusters quite heavily in the center and the AAV sort of spreads

14:29.360 --> 14:33.640
out around the sides.

14:33.640 --> 14:41.720
But yes, what we didn't want to do was to run all of this data through ESM2 to plot the

14:41.720 --> 14:47.800
AAV data compared to the ESM2 data because then you've, I mean it's very difficult to

14:47.800 --> 14:53.080
tell whether that, it falls out of distribution or not when doing that.

14:53.080 --> 14:57.760
Hi, Dan Cox, thank you for the talk.

14:57.760 --> 15:01.440
One question, when you said you fine-tuned the model after the mass language modeling

15:01.440 --> 15:08.320
on specific tasks, you were meaning transduction tasks, a series of tasks?

15:08.320 --> 15:12.280
Right, yeah, so we have calculated various different endpoints.

15:12.280 --> 15:19.880
The example I showed here is we take the number of variants that we find in just the virus

15:19.880 --> 15:25.440
before it goes into tissue and then we check the log fold change after we sequenced the

15:25.440 --> 15:30.160
tissue and then compare it relative to wild type.

15:30.160 --> 15:38.120
Would you try it on multiple tissues at once?

15:38.120 --> 15:42.600
So we have done that for the multitask model, we use all the data at once there.

15:42.600 --> 15:48.080
We give the model information about which tissue that data has come from with the idea

15:48.080 --> 15:52.760
being that if it has that small amount of information it might be able to compare sequences

15:52.760 --> 15:55.920
across tissues at the same time.

15:55.920 --> 16:01.480
I haven't tried blindly using all of the data from all the tissues sort of unlabeled

16:01.480 --> 16:02.480
as one big data set.

16:02.480 --> 16:04.600
I think it would be quite interesting to try though.

16:04.600 --> 16:06.240
I see, thank you.

16:06.240 --> 16:08.120
Thank you.

16:08.120 --> 16:11.760
Thank you, Andrew.

16:11.760 --> 16:14.920
Thank you very much.

16:14.920 --> 16:20.360
So, this is the last one.

